Thanks very much Finn, this was very helpful in pointing me where I needed to go!

Just to update in case this topic can help someone else in the future, removing the integrate-to-zero constraint (which I applied wrongly not understanding what it did), separating the spatial and temporal parts of the model by removing the group by week in the A matrix and instead fitting an RW1 model for week, and then using inla.rerun() on the resulting model solved both the problem of the unreasonably large spatial effect magnitude and the unrealistic spatial range. The priors were unchanged. The unfortunate side effect was that now rather than getting a separate spatial effect graph for each week, I only have one. Small sacrifice for a model that makes sense!

For reference, here's the new formula:

formula <- formula <- y ~ 0 + Prcp + Tavg + DevHi + NDVI + Builtb41950 + Built50to69 + Built70to89 + Built90to00 + BasinArea + Year +
  f(spatial, model=spde) + f(Week, model = 'rw1', scale.model= TRUE, hyper = list(theta=list(prior="pc.prec", param= c(0.5,0.01))))

And the new spatial effect mean plot (units in log-odds), which makes much more sense in terms of scale and distribution for my data:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-sHPiebGj72E/WlOFNbMQqCI/AAAAAAAAAEM/l1UPurw4ri4RShq7TyOIjBGGwjFfbXafgCLcBGAs/s1600/newspatialcapture.JPG>

---- Mark

On Wednesday, January 3, 2018 at 4:34:25 PM UTC-5, Finn Lindgren wrote:

> Hi,
>
> to answer the easy question first, no, having multiple observations in the same location is not a problem.
>
> It looks like you have non-identifiability issues between the covariates and the spatio-temporal effect. Note that imposing the integrate-to zero constraint changes the model from a regular Matern model, so that the interpretation of the sigma-parameter isn't quite the same any more.  It's hard to tell the spatial scale of the figures, but it seems to me that if the SPDE model is intended to pick up residual spatiotemporal structure not explained by the covariates, perhaps the 10 km prior median range is a bit big?
>
> Have you run the model without this component and looked at some kind of spatial residual plots?  If there are large-scale spatial or temporal trends, you might add that as explicit covariates.
> In fact, looking at the final plots, it seems to me that the data in the middle has a temporally increasing trend, and due to the spatial integrate-to-zero constraint, it therefore has to let the surrounding unobserved regions have a decreasing trend to compensate!
> Adding an explicit linear trend (or possibly a separate rw1 or rw2 model) by week) might resolve the issue.
>
> Finn
>

